    "description": "Shahr-e Sukhteh, also recorded by UNESCO as Shahr-i Sokhta, is a Bronze Age archaeological city in southeastern Iran. Its mudbrick buildings, workshops and cemetery preserve evidence of urban life, specialized crafts and contacts with neighboring regions.",
    "tags": ["Bronze Age", "Sistan", "Urban archaeology", "World Heritage", "Craft production"],
    "status": "UNESCO World Heritage Site",
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T11:53:10Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T11:53:10Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Shahr-e Sukhteh: Urban Life in Iran's Bronze Age Burnt City | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the buildings, workshops and archaeological uncertainties of Shahr-e Sukhteh, a UNESCO-listed Bronze Age city in Sistan."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "city-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the Burnt City",
        "content": "Shahr-e Sukhteh means “Burnt City,” but it is a later name rather than an established ancient identification. The archaeological site lies in Sistan and Baluchestan Province in southeastern Iran. UNESCO summarizes its occupation as beginning around 3200 BCE and continuing through successive phases until approximately 1800 BCE. These rounded dates describe a long settlement history, not a single city preserved at one moment. [s1][s2]\n\nIts importance lies in the combination of evidence. Mudbrick houses, substantial building complexes, manufacturing areas and a cemetery allow researchers to investigate how people inhabited, worked in and organized an early city. UNESCO inscribed the site on the World Heritage List in 2014. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "urban-life",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Homes, workshops and burial grounds",
        "content": "The physical remains make urban life tangible. The ICOMOS evaluation describes courtyard houses connected by alleyways, with spaces for cooking, keeping animals and sometimes craft activities. ICOMOS is the International Council on Monuments and Sites, an advisory body that evaluates cultural World Heritage nominations. Its account distinguishes residential quarters from monumental buildings, industrial areas and burial grounds. [s2]\n\nUNESCO identifies metalworking, stone vessels, gems and pottery as important elements of the city's archaeological record. These activities place skilled production alongside domestic life in the interpretation of the settlement. The surviving material is therefore valuable for understanding work and social organization, as well as artistic accomplishment. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "connections",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connections without an assumed trading empire",
        "content": "UNESCO's adopted statement emphasizes contacts reaching the Indus Plain, Central Asia and other parts of Iran. The scale and organization of those connections require more caution. In its evaluation, ICOMOS questioned whether the nomination demonstrated a centralized trading network or sustained, large-scale long-distance commerce. It distinguished evidence of contact from stronger claims about commercial dominance. [s1][s2]\n\nThis distinction helps readers assess spectacular discoveries: an imported material or shared object type can demonstrate a connection without establishing direct trade, political control or population movement. The evaluation also challenged speculative statements about the city's capital status and ethnic diversity. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "abandonment-and-preservation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Water, abandonment and preservation",
        "content": "UNESCO associates the city's eventual abandonment with altered watercourses and climatic change. Its dry setting subsequently helped preserve archaeological remains, but exposed mudbrick still requires maintenance. The World Heritage account describes protective applications of earthen plaster to excavated walls. Preservation is an ongoing task rather than an automatic consequence of desert conditions. [s1]\n\nThe most useful reading of Shahr-e Sukhteh holds achievement and uncertainty together: a substantial early city is securely documented, while the reach of its institutions and exchange networks remains open to investigation. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
