    "description": "Marlik is an excavated elite cemetery in the Rudbar area of Gilan, near Iran's southwestern Caspian region. Its vessels, ornaments, weapons and figurines illuminate a society of the late second and early first millennia BCE, although its political identity and detailed chronology remain debated.",
    "tags": ["Marlik", "Gilan", "Iron Age", "Burial archaeology", "Metalworking"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T11:53:10Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T11:53:10Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Marlik Civilization: The Cemetery, Craftsmanship and Dating Debate | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover what Marlik's excavated tombs reveal about craftsmanship and social distinction in ancient Gilan, and where interpretation remains uncertain."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "cemetery-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A cemetery as a window onto society",
        "content": "The title “Marlik Civilization” refers here to the society known through an elite cemetery, rather than a securely identified ancient state. Marlik stands in the Gowharrud valley in the Rudbar area of Gilan. Its archaeological setting belongs to the foothills south of the Caspian Sea, a landscape quite different from the desert settlements of southeastern Iran. [s1][s2]\n\nEzzatollah Negahban led the principal excavation from October 1961 to November 1962, working with the Iranian Archaeological Service and the University of Tehran. The discovery mattered because objects from northern Iran were already circulating through the antiquities market under broad labels that concealed their actual origins. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "objects-and-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "More than a collection of precious objects",
        "content": "The excavation documented 53 tombs, with differences in construction and contents. Some were roughly built; others used carefully arranged stonework, including stone brought from elsewhere in the valley. Such differences provide evidence for social distinction, although burial wealth does not supply a complete map of the living community. [s1]\n\nNegahban's account describes gold, silver and bronze vessels, ornaments, weapons, tools and human and animal figurines. Metalworkers used techniques including engraving and repoussé, in which relief decoration is formed by working metal from behind. Ceramic figures of humped bulls are particularly characteristic, alongside representations of other animals. The range of materials and techniques makes craftsmanship central to understanding Marlik. [s2]\n\nCharles K. Wilkinson's early museum study emphasized the value of objects recorded in their original positions. Their associations allow archaeologists to study combinations of pottery, weapons and ornaments rather than treating each piece as an isolated artwork. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "dating-and-identity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Dating the burials and interpreting rank",
        "content": "Marlik's chronology remains debated. Kamyar Abdi's scholarly synthesis reports substantial acceptance of Negahban's proposed use between the fourteenth and tenth centuries BCE, while allowing that some burials may be later. Those boundaries should be understood as an archaeological interpretation, not exact opening and closing dates. [s1]\n\nThe familiar description “royal cemetery” is also interpretive. Rich graves and weapons support discussion of elites, but they do not independently identify named rulers or a dynasty. Poor skeletal preservation restricts assessment of the occupants' age and sex. Claims about particular political offices or identities therefore require more evidence than valuable grave goods alone. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-significance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why the excavation still matters",
        "content": "“Amlash” was widely used for northern Iranian antiquities whose precise origins were uncertain. Wilkinson warned that it covered objects from different, sometimes unknown sites. Marlik's documented excavation offers a firmer foundation for comparison. [s3]\n\nIts lasting contribution is methodological as well as artistic: the relationship between objects, tombs and landscape preserves information that an attractive object without a recorded findspot cannot recover. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
